\DOC SIMPLIFY_MAYCHANGES_TAC

\TYPE {SIMPLIFY_MAYCHANGES_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Rewrites a {MAYCHANGE ... s0 sN} assumption into a compacted, normalised form,
leaving the conclusion untouched.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. The tactic finds the assumption stating a {,,}-composition
of many singleton {MAYCHANGE} components relating an initial and a current
state, and replaces it by a single equivalent assumption in which components of
the same kind are merged: separate register/flag components become one
{MAYCHANGE} over a list of components, and adjacent small memory components are
coalesced into larger contiguous byte-range components (absorbing an
already-coalesced range if one is present).
{
   A, (MAYCHANGE c1 ,, ... ,, MAYCHANGE cn) s t ?- u
   ================================================== SIMPLIFY_MAYCHANGES_TAC
   A, (MAYCHANGE d1 ,, ... ,, MAYCHANGE dm) s t ?- u
}
\noindent Here the {d} components are the merged groups; the memory ones may be
absent when the original has no memory components.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of assumption rewriting, always
on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about machine execution.

\ENDDOC
